note: "NO T2 WRITE-BACK, AND THAT IS THE CORRECT OUTCOME RATHER THAN AN OMISSION. A design run's output is the run; nothing it produced is yet true of the code. docs/use-cases/web-ui.html is NOT touched: no observable contract moved, because zero ui/src/ files changed. UC-4's three gains that W2's fix landed early — statuses carrying their reason phrase, the dot's 24px target, its accessible name — remain W4's row to state once, when the rest of its migration lands. Splitting one UC row across two WUs is how a use-case doc becomes a changelog, and W2's result held them back for exactly this reason. docs/design-rules/design-rules-index.html is NOT touched, deliberately and twice over: the rulebook is FROZEN INPUT for a design run (DEC-14, and the flow spec's 'the substrate is an input, frozen per run'), and three more screen runs (W5/W7/W9) must still pin the same blob. This run's wrap-not-truncate finding is real and belongs in the rulebook, but editing it mid-epic would change a substrate those runs read. It is carried on the run's own blocked-on list, addressed to W11, where the epic's distillation plan already reserves the rulebook row for W2's held chromatic-meanings entry. No T1 edit: the flow, rulebook and gate are inputs to this epic. THREE ITEMS ARE HANDED FORWARD ON THE RUN'S BLOCKED-ON LIST, and each names whose call it is rather than being left as a loose end. (1) wrap-not-truncate has no clause for a fixed-height virtualized row — W11, the rulebook's own distillation. (2) The method and status filters are exact matches behind a free-text box, so 'GE' or '4' return an empty list indistinguishable from no traffic; three options are recorded UNPICKED because the only one that makes 4xx expressible needs FlowFilter.status to accept a class, which is an API contract outside this screen — owner's call, then W4 or later. (3) The listbox keyboard contract is designed but unproven — no shipped list in this app has one, so the up/down-without-wrap convention is borrowed from Select's options rather than from a precedent at list scale; W4 finds out at build. THE HANDOVER TO W4 IS THE POINT OF THIS WU. The run is the design W4 must cite by blob in design.implements {path, blob, built} — the citation path DEC-28 fixed and W2 first exercised. W4 builds from it without a design decision of its own: the components, their placement, the states, the keyboard paths and the microcopy are all named, including the one interaction the current screen cannot perform at all — row selection has no keyboard path today, the row being a div with an onClick."
